    id: "rc-invisibility",
    name: "Invisibility",
    section: "I",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "Invisibility makes a creature or object undetectable by vision, including darkvision, but invisible targets can be sensed in other ways. Spot: an observer can notice the presence of an active invisible target within 30 feet by succeeding on a Spot check (DC = the invisible creature's Hide check +20), gaining a hunch that 'something's there' without being able to see or accurately target it; an invisible creature holding still is harder to notice (DC = its Hide check +30); an invisible inanimate object or an invisible, unliving creature holding still is nearly impossible to notice (DC 40 for an invisible object; DC = Hide check +40 for an invisible creature); a helpless invisible creature is treated as an invisible inanimate object (DC 40); a Spot check that beats the DC by 20 pinpoints the target's location, but the target still benefits from total concealment. Listen: an observer can use hearing against an invisible target that is making any sound, making a Listen check as a free action each round; a result at least equal to the invisible creature's Move Silently check reveals its presence ('over there somewhere'), and beating the DC by 20 pinpoints its location, though it still benefits from total concealment. DCs to hear: an invisible creature in combat or speaking — DC 0; moving at up to half speed — its Move Silently check result; moving at more than half speed — its Move Silently result -5; running or charging — its Move Silently result -20; modifiers: +1 per 10 feet of distance, +5 when behind an obstacle such as a door, +15 behind a stone wall; for an invisible object, the DM sets the DC according to the Listen skill. Touch: an observer can feel around for an invisible target by making a touch attack with appendages or a weapon into two adjacent 5-foot squares as a standard action; if the target is in the designated area, the touch attack must hit despite total concealment, and a hit deals no damage but pinpoints the target's current location — if the target then moves, its location is unknown again. Those struck by an invisible attacker know the attacker's location, unless the attack came from farther than 5 feet away, in which case they know the general location but have not pinpointed it; if the invisible attacker moves, its location is once again unknown. Special senses: scent lets a creature smell an invisible target as easily as a visible one; blindsight allows interaction with invisible targets as if visible; the Blind-Fight feat makes a creature more effective against invisible attackers; invisibility does not thwart detect spells. Harming: attacking an invisible target whose location has been pinpointed resolves normally except that the target benefits from total concealment (a particularly large and slow target might receive a smaller or no miss chance, at the DM's option); attacking a target whose location has not been pinpointed, the attacker chooses the space the attack is directed at — if the target is there, the attack resolves with the usual total-concealment miss chance, and if it is not there and the attacker is a PC, the DM rolls the miss chance as if the target were there, does not reveal the result, and tells the player the character missed, so the player cannot tell whether the miss was absence or miss chance. Attacks: an invisible attacker gains a +2 bonus on attack rolls against opponents that cannot see it, and opponents are denied their Dexterity bonuses to AC against its attacks; invisible creatures cannot use gaze attacks. Environment: an invisible target in water displaces water, revealing its location, though it is still hard to see and benefits from concealment. Other considerations: an invisible creature that picks up a visible object leaves that object visible, though it can hide a small visible item under clothing or in a similar place, rendering the object effectively invisible; an invisible burning torch still gives off light, as does an invisible object with a light spell (or similar spell) cast on it; invisible creatures leave tracks and can be tracked normally, and footprints in sand, mud, or other soft surfaces can clue enemies in to their location. Ethereal creatures are invisible, but since they are not materially present, Spot checks, Listen checks, scent, Blind-Fight, and blindsight do not help locate them; incorporeal creatures can be invisible, and Blind-Fight, blindsight, and scent do not help find or attack an invisible incorporeal creature, though Spot checks can help, and Listen checks do not help unless the creature wants to be heard.",
