    id: "rc-language",
    name: "Language",
    section: "L",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "All characters can speak Common; nonhumans also speak a racial language, and some classes give access to languages. Common languages and their alphabets: Abyssal (Infernal), Aquan (Elven), Auran (Draconic), Celestial (Celestial), Common (Common), Draconic (Draconic), Druidic (Druidic), Dwarven (Dwarven), Elven (Elven), Giant (Dwarven), Gnome (Dwarven), Goblin (Dwarven), Gnoll (Common), Halfling (Common), Ignan (Draconic), Infernal (Infernal), Orc (Dwarven), Sylvan (Elven), Terran (Dwarven), and Undercommon (Elven); other languages exist, spoken by particular kinds of creatures. Literate characters can read and write all the languages they speak. DECIPHER SCRIPT (Int; trained only): take 1 minute to decipher writing in an unfamiliar language or a message in an incomplete or archaic form; DCs are 20 for simple text, 25 for standard text, and 30 for exotic or old text. The DM makes the check; on a success you understand the general content of about a page of writing (or the equivalent); on a failure you must succeed on a DC 5 Wisdom check (also made secretly by the DM) or draw a false conclusion about the text, and if you fail you cannot try again. Creating a cipher: you can use Decipher Script to create a private cipher in a week; the code lets you (or anyone with the proper key) record information others cannot readily read; others with ranks in Decipher Script can attempt to decode it at a DC of 10 + your total skill modifier at the time you created the cipher, with the first attempt requiring a day of uninterrupted work and each subsequent retry a week. FORGERY (Int): use this skill to fake documents or detect forgeries; you must be able to read and write the required language. Making a forgery requires appropriate writing materials, sufficient visual acuity, wax for seals if appropriate, and about 1 minute for a very short and simple document; a longer or more complex document takes 1d4 minutes per page. To forge a document whose handwriting is not specific to a person, you need only have seen a similar document before and gain a +8 bonus on the check; to forge a signature you need an appropriate signature to copy and gain +4; to forge a longer document written as if by a particular person, a large sample of that person's handwriting is needed. The Forgery check is made secretly and is opposed by the Forgery check of whoever examines the document for authenticity; the same check result stands for that document whenever a different examiner reads it, and no examiner can attempt to detect a particular forgery more than once. Examiner modifiers: only casually reviews the document -2; does not know the handwriting -2; intimately knows the handwriting +2; does not know the document type -2; intimately knows the document type +2. A document that contradicts procedure, orders, or previous knowledge, or that requires sacrifice from the person checking it, can increase suspicion, and at the DM's option increased suspicion creates more favorable circumstances for the examiner's opposing check. A retry is never possible after a particular examiner detects a particular forgery, though the document might still fool someone else; when a forgery is detected, the bearer often suffers legal troubles or worse. Favorable forgeries: at the DM's discretion, forged documents can grant a +2 circumstance bonus on a specific Bluff, Diplomacy, or Intimidate check when you present them and the creature you are interacting with does not detect the forgery; if the documents are detected, the check they would have modified automatically fails.",
