    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "Light sources illuminate a radius of bright illumination and, beyond it, shadowy illumination, for a duration: candle — no bright radius, shadowy 5 feet, 1 hour (a candle provides only shadowy illumination); everburning torch — bright 20 feet, shadowy 40 feet, permanent; lamp, common — bright 15 feet, shadowy 30 feet, 6 hours per pint of oil; lantern, bullseye — a bright 60-foot cone, shadowy 120-foot cone, 6 hours per pint (a bullseye lantern illuminates a cone, not a radius); lantern, hooded — bright 30 feet, shadowy 60 feet, 6 hours per pint; sunrod — bright 30 feet, shadowy 60 feet, 6 hours; torch — bright 20 feet, shadowy 40 feet, 1 hour. Light spells: continual flame — bright 20 feet, shadowy 40 feet, permanent; dancing lights — bright 20 feet (each light), shadowy 40 feet (each), 1 minute; daylight — bright 60 feet, shadowy 120 feet, 30 minutes; light — bright 20 feet, shadowy 40 feet, 10 minutes. Bright light: most creatures can see clearly; a creature cannot hide in an area of bright light unless it is invisible, has concealment, or has cover. Shadowy illumination: most creatures can see dimly, and creatures within the area have concealment against those who can see only dimly. Darkness: in areas of darkness, creatures without senses that can overcome the darkness are effectively blinded. Distant light: in open spaces a light source can be spotted from much farther than its radius of illumination — an observer who succeeds on a DC 20 Spot check sees the light from a distance of 20 times its radius of illumination if the observer is in complete darkness, or 10 times its radius if the observer is in dim light, and an observer who fails the check automatically notices the light source at half that range. Observers outside a light source's illumination radius can see into the illumination just fine, making Spot checks as normal to discern creatures or objects in the illuminated area.",
