    id: "rc-mounted-combat",
    name: "Mounted Combat",
    section: "M",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "RIDE (Dex; armor check penalty applies): you can ride many creatures suitable as mounts; riding a creature ill suited as a mount imposes a -5 penalty on Ride checks, and riding without a saddle imposes a -5 penalty. Typical maneuvers such as saddling, mounting, riding, and dismounting require no checks. Ride tasks and DCs — no action required: cover 15; enter water 10; guide with knees 5; leap 15; soft fall 15; stay in saddle 5; swim while mounted (see below). Free action: fast mount or dismount 20; fight as war mount attacks 10. Move action: control nonwar mount in battle 20; spur mount 15. Cover: drop down and hang alongside your mount, using it as cover; make the Ride check before the opponent makes its attack roll, and if multiple opponents attack on the same initiative count, make one check for cover against all of them; you cannot claim this cover while attacking, casting spells, or charging, and cannot attack, cast spells, or charge while using your mount as cover; on a failure you gain no cover. Enter water: failure means the land mount refuses to enter the water, and failure by 5 or more means the mount balks, throwing you into the water while it stays ashore; on an aquatic mount, failure means a -5 penalty on your next Ride check to stay astride the swimming mount, and failure by 5 or more means you are unsaddled as the mount enters the water. Guide with knees: make the check at the start of your turn to guide the mount with your knees and use both hands in combat; on a failure you can use only one hand this round. Leap: use your Ride modifier or the mount's Jump modifier, whichever is lower, to determine how far the mount leaps; on a failure you fall off the mount when it leaps and take the appropriate falling damage (at least 1d6 points). Soft fall: try to take no damage when you fall off a mount; on a failure you take 1d6 points of damage. Stay in saddle: avoid falling when your mount rears or bolts unexpectedly or when you take damage. Swim while mounted: each round your mount swims, make a Ride check to stay mounted — DC 10 for calm water, 15 for rough water or underwater, and 20 for stormy water; on a failure your mount swims at half speed and takes a -5 penalty on Swim checks until you succeed on another Ride check; on a failure by 5 or more you are unsaddled, and getting mounted again requires a successful Swim check at the water's DC and a Ride check at the same DC. Fast mount or dismount: you can attempt it with a mount up to one size category larger than yourself as a free action, provided you still have a move action available that round; on a failure, mounting or dismounting is a move action. Fight as war mount attacks: if you direct your war-trained mount to attack in battle, you can still make your attacks normally. Control nonwar mount in battle: attempt to control a mount not trained for combat while riding it in battle; on a failure the move action becomes a full-round action and you can do nothing else until your next turn. Spur mount: a successful check increases the mount's speed by 10 feet for 1 round but deals 1 point of damage to the creature; it can be used every round, but each consecutive round of additional speed deals twice as much damage as the previous round. Rider unconscious: if you are knocked unconscious you have a 50% chance to stay in the saddle (75% in a military saddle); otherwise you fall and take 1d6 points of damage, and without you to guide it your mount avoids combat. Combat while mounted: your mount acts on your initiative count as you direct it; the mount uses its action to move and you move along with it; when mounted, you share your mount's space completely, and multiple riders share their mount's space completely as well. Moving: if your mount moves more than 5 feet, you can make only a single melee attack — you must wait until the mount gets to your enemy, so you cannot make a full attack; even at your mount's full speed you take no penalty on melee attacks while mounted; you can take your own move actions normally, such as loading and firing a light crossbow while your mount moves. Charging: if your mount charges, you also take the AC penalty of a charge, and if you attack at the end of the charge you receive the charge's bonus. Higher ground: when you attack a creature on foot that is smaller than your mount, you gain a +1 bonus on melee attack rolls for being on higher ground, and the same bonus applies if the DM determines you are higher than your target for some other reason. Overrun: when you attempt an overrun while mounted, your mount makes the Strength check and applies its size modifier rather than yours; if you knock the defender prone, your mount can make a single attack against that creature with a natural weapon on its front feet (not a special attack) — if the mount has no natural weapons on its feet, it cannot make this attack. Casting spells: you can cast normally if your mount moves only its speed or less before or after you cast; if your mount double moves, it is moving both before and after, so you are casting while it moves and must make a Concentration check for vigorous motion (DC 10 + spell level) or lose the spell; if your mount runs, you cast when it has moved up to twice its speed, and the Concentration check is for violent motion (DC 15 + spell level). Shooting: if your mount moves only its speed or less, you take no penalty on ranged attack rolls; you can use ranged weapons while your mount double moves at a -4 penalty on attack rolls, or while it runs at a -8 penalty instead; in either of those cases you make the attack rolls when your mount has completed half its movement, measuring range from that point; you can make a full attack with a ranged weapon while your mount is moving.",
