    id: "rc-natural-attacks",
    name: "Natural Attacks",
    section: "N",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "Natural attacks come in two forms — natural weapons, which are physically part of a creature, and special attacks, which are special ways a creature uses its inborn attributes to harm others; individual monster descriptions take precedence over these general rules. Attacks: a creature making a melee attack with a natural weapon is considered armed and does not provoke attacks of opportunity, and it threatens any space it can reach; unless otherwise noted, a natural weapon threatens a critical hit on a natural attack roll of 20. Creatures do not receive additional attacks from a high base attack bonus when using natural weapons — the number of attacks depends on the type: one bite attack, one attack per claw or tentacle, one gore attack, one sting attack, or one slam attack; Large or larger creatures with arms or armlike limbs can make a slam attack with each arm. Primary and secondary: when a creature has more than one natural weapon, one of them (or sometimes a pair or set) is the primary weapon and the rest are secondary. The primary weapon uses the creature's full attack bonus and applies its full Strength modifier on damage — or 1-1/2 x its Strength bonus if it is the creature's only natural weapon; a Strength penalty on damage applies whenever a natural weapon is used but is never multiplied. Secondary natural weapon attacks are made at a -5 penalty on the attack roll no matter how many the creature has, even on a single attack or an attack of opportunity, and apply 1/2 the Strength bonus to damage (but the full Strength penalty), even when a secondary weapon is used alone as a single attack. Weapon types: Bite — attacks with the mouth, dealing piercing, slashing, and bludgeoning damage. Claw or talon — rips with a sharp appendage, dealing piercing and slashing damage. Gore — spears with an antler, horn, or similar appendage, dealing piercing damage. Slap or slam — batters with an appendage, dealing bludgeoning damage. Sting — stabs with a stinger, dealing piercing damage, and usually poison damage in addition to hit point damage. Tentacle — flails with a powerful tentacle, dealing bludgeoning damage, and sometimes slashing damage in addition. Combining weapons: when a creature combines manufactured and natural weapons in a full attack, the manufactured weapon attack is the primary attack (unless the description indicates otherwise) and any natural weapons also used are secondary, taking the usual secondary penalty even if the natural weapon is normally the creature's primary; these secondary attacks do not interfere with the primary attack. Special attacks: Aligned strike (supernatural) — the creature's attacks are treated as aligned (chaotic, evil, good, or lawful, as noted) for overcoming damage reduction. Constrict (extraordinary) — after a successful grapple check to deal damage, the creature crushes the opponent for bludgeoning damage in the amount given in its entry; combined with improved grab, constrict damage is dealt in addition to the damage of the weapon used to grab. Disease (extraordinary) — the creature can infect another creature with disease; diseases can be extraordinary or supernatural even if the inflicting special attack is extraordinary. Epic strike (supernatural) — natural weapon attacks are treated as having a +6 magical enhancement bonus for overcoming damage reduction. Improved grab (extraordinary) — when the creature hits with a natural melee weapon (usually a claw or bite), it deals normal damage and attempts to start a grapple as a free action without provoking attacks of opportunity and with no initial melee touch attack required; unless otherwise noted it works only against opponents at least one size category smaller. On gaining a hold, the creature pulls the opponent into its space without provoking attacks of opportunity, and can conduct the grapple normally or simply hold the opponent with the body part used — choosing the latter imposes a -20 penalty on grapple checks, but the creature is not considered grappled itself, is not denied its Dexterity bonus to AC, still threatens an area, and can use its remaining attacks against other opponents. Whenever it succeeds on a grapple check to deal damage, it deals the damage of the natural weapon used for the improved grab, plus constrict damage if it also has constrict. Magic strike (supernatural) — natural weapon attacks are treated as magic for overcoming damage reduction. Paralysis — the creature can render another creature paralyzed; this special attack can be extraordinary or supernatural. Poison (extraordinary) — the creature can poison another creature. Pounce (extraordinary) — when the creature charges, it can still make a full attack even if it charged while restricted to a single action; all its attacks receive the +2 charge bonus on attack rolls, and if it uses its attacks to successfully start a grapple and has the rake ability, it can also make rake attacks. Powerful charge (extraordinary) — on a charge, the creature's attack deals extra damage in addition to the normal benefits and hazards of a charge (similar to, but not the same as, the feat of the same name). Rake (extraordinary) — the creature gains extra natural attacks when it grapples, usually two additional claw attacks usable only against a grappled foe; rake attacks are not subject to the usual -4 penalty for attacking with a natural weapon in a grapple and use the creature's normal attack bonus, dealing normal damage for the natural weapon used + 1/2 the creature's Strength bonus or its full Strength penalty; a monster with rake must begin its turn grappling to use it, and cannot begin a grapple and rake in the same turn unless it has pounce or a similar ability. Rend (extraordinary) — if the creature hits with the specified natural attack, it latches on and tears the flesh, dealing damage equal to that natural weapon's damage + 1-1/2 times its Strength bonus or its full Strength penalty. Swallow whole (extraordinary) — if the creature begins its turn with an opponent held in its mouth, it can attempt a new grapple check; on a success it swallows the foe, dealing normal bite damage; the opponent must usually be smaller than the swallowing creature. A swallowed opponent is considered grappled, but the swallowing creature is not; the swallowed opponent can try to cut its way free with any light slashing or piercing weapon, or try to escape the grapple; the inside of a creature normally has an Armor Class of 5 + 1/2 its natural armor bonus, with no modifiers for size or Dexterity; if the swallowed opponent escapes the grapple, it ends up back in the attacker's mouth, where it can be bitten or swallowed again. Trample (extraordinary) — as a full-round action, the creature can move up to twice its speed and run over any opponents at least one size category smaller than it; it merely has to move over the opponents in its path, and any creature whose space is completely covered by the trampling creature's space is subject to the attack; if a target's space is larger than 5 feet, it is trampled only if the trampling creature moves over all the squares it occupies, and if only some of its space is covered, the target can make an attack of opportunity at a -4 penalty. Trample deals bludgeoning damage as given in the creature's description (usually its slam damage + 1-1/2 x its Strength bonus or its full Strength penalty). Trampled opponents can attempt attacks of opportunity at a -4 penalty on the attack roll; if they do not, they can attempt Reflex saves to take half damage, with a save DC of 10 + 1/2 the creature's HD + its Strength modifier (the exact DC is given in the creature's descriptive text). A creature can deal trample damage to each target only once per round, no matter how many times its movement takes it over that target.",
