    id: "rc-overrun",
    name: "Overrun",
    section: "O",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "You can attempt an overrun as a standard action taken during your movement — an exception to the rule that you cannot take a standard action during movement. With an overrun, you attempt to plow past or over a defender and move through that creature's space as you move. You can overrun a defender who is one size category larger than you or smaller, and you can make only one overrun attempt per round. Initiating: you begin by moving into the defender's space, so you provoke an attack of opportunity from the defender, and you also provoke attacks of opportunity as normal for your movement. Resolving: the defender can avoid you or block you. If the defender avoids you, it suffers no ill effect and you can keep moving, since you can always move through a square occupied by someone who lets you by; the overrun does not cost a standard action in that case, but the movement required to enter the defender's space counts against your movement for the round. If the defender blocks you, make a Strength check opposed by the defender's Dexterity or Strength check — whichever ability score has the higher modifier; a combatant gets a +4 bonus on the check for every size category it is larger than Medium or a -4 penalty for every size category it is smaller than Medium, and the defender gets a +4 bonus if it has more than two legs or is otherwise more stable than a normal humanoid. If you win, you knock the defender prone, and it must use a move action that provokes attacks of opportunity to stand up again. If you lose, the defender can immediately react and make a Strength check opposed by your Dexterity or Strength check — including the size modifiers noted above, but no other modifiers — to try to knock you prone. Results: if you succeed in knocking the defender prone, you can continue your movement as normal. If you fail and are knocked prone in turn, you must move 5 feet back the way you came and fall prone, ending your movement there. If you fail but are not knocked prone, you must move 5 feet back the way you came, ending your movement there; if that square is occupied, you fall prone in that square.",
