    section: "S",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "When you are subjected to an unusual or magical attack, you make a saving throw (or save) to avoid or reduce the effect. Like an attack roll, a saving throw is a d20 roll plus modifiers: saving throw bonus = base save bonus + ability modifier + racial bonus + resistance bonus + other modifiers. Success and failure: if the result equals or exceeds the save's DC, the saving throw succeeds, and the DC is determined by the attack. A creature that successfully saves against an effect with no obvious physical repercussions feels a hostile force or a tingle but cannot deduce the exact nature of the attack; likewise, if a creature's saving throw succeeds against a targeted effect, the effect's creator senses that the effect has failed — a caster cannot sense when creatures succeed on saves against effect spells or area spells, or similar abilities. Automatic failure and success: a natural 1 on a saving throw is always a failure, and the attack that forced the save might cause damage to exposed items (see below); a natural 20 is always a success. Voluntarily failing: a creature can voluntarily forgo a saving throw and willingly accept a consequence. Items surviving: unless an effect's descriptive text specifies otherwise, all items carried or worn by a creature are assumed to survive a magical attack; if a creature rolls a natural 1 on its saving throw against the effect, however, an exposed item is harmed if the attack can harm objects — determine which four objects carried or worn by the creature are most likely to be affected and roll randomly among them, and the randomly determined item must make a saving throw against the attack form and, if the save fails, take whatever damage the attack deals. If an item is not carried or worn, and is not magical, it does not get a saving throw — it simply takes the appropriate damage. The order of likelihood for items affected by magical attacks, from most likely to least: 1st shield; 2nd armor; 3rd magic helmet, hat, or headband; 4th item in hand (including a weapon, wand, or the like); 5th magic cloak; 6th stowed or sheathed weapon; 7th magic bracers; 8th magic clothing; 9th magic jewelry (including rings); 10th anything else. The three saving throw types: Fortitude saves reflect physical toughness — stamina, ruggedness, physique, bulk, metabolism, resistance, and immunity — and apply your Constitution modifier. Reflex saves reflect physical (and sometimes mental) agility — quickness, nimbleness, hand-eye coordination, overall coordination, speed, and reaction time — and apply your Dexterity modifier; you can make a Reflex save whenever one is called for, but your Dexterity or whether you can apply its modifier might be altered by the situation. Will saves reflect inner strength — willpower, mental stability, power of the mind, levelheadedness, determination, self-confidence, self-awareness, the superego, and resistance to temptation — and apply your Wisdom modifier. Against spells: the Saving Throw line in a spell description defines which type of saving throw the spell allows and how saves against it work; spell saving throw DC = 10 + the level of the spell + the bonus for the spellcaster's relevant ability score; a spell's level can vary depending on your class, so always use the spell level applicable to your class, and the relevant ability score varies by spellcasting class. Against magic items: most magic item descriptions give saving throw DCs for the item's effects; if not, magic item saving throw DC = 10 + (1-1/2 x the level of the spell or effect); staffs and runestaffs (Magic Item Compendium, p. 223) are exceptions — treat their saving throw DCs as if the wielder had cast the spell, including caster level and all modifiers to the save DC. Against special attacks: any sort of special ability — extraordinary, spell-like, or supernatural — might allow a saving throw; special ability saving throw DC = 10 + 1/2 the creature's HD + the modifier for the ability score on which the ability is based + other modifiers (often racial); spell-like abilities are an exception — spell-like ability saving throw DC = 10 + the level of the spell the ability resembles or duplicates + the creature's Charisma modifier + other modifiers (often racial), where spell level is usually the sorcerer/wizard version of the spell, or, if the spell has no such version, the version that makes the most sense for the creature. Terms for saves, commonly used in spell descriptions, class features, and monster special abilities: Negates — the effect has no consequences for a subject that succeeds. Partial — the effect has consequences, diminished on a successful save as detailed in the description. Half — the effect deals damage, and a successful save halves it. None — no saving throw is allowed. Disbelief — a successful save lets the subject ignore the effect. (object) — the effect can be used on objects, which receive saving throws only if they are magical or attended by a creature resisting the effect, in which case the object uses the creature's saving throw bonus unless its own bonus is greater; a magic item's saving throw bonuses each equal 2 + 1/2 the item's caster level; this notation does not mean the effect can be used only on objects, since some such effects can be cast on creatures and/or objects. (harmless) — the effect is usually beneficial, but a targeted creature can attempt a saving throw if it desires. Special save abilities: Evasion and improved evasion allow the target of an area attack to leap or twist out of the way — if a creature with evasion is subjected to an attack that allows a Reflex save for half damage, it takes no damage on a successful save; improved evasion is the same except that even on a failed saving throw the creature takes only half damage. A creature must have room to move to evade — a bound creature or one squeezing through an area cannot use evasion. Evasion and improved evasion are reflexive: the creature need not know the attack is coming. Sometimes a creature with evasion or improved evasion cannot use the ability while wearing medium or heavy armor, but only if the ability's description says so. Mettle allows a creature to shrug off magical effects that are reduced in efficacy on a successful Will or Fortitude save (such as a spell with a saving throw entry of Will partial or Fortitude half): on a successful save, a creature with mettle instead negates such an effect entirely; an unconscious or sleeping creature does not gain the benefit of mettle.",
    source: "Rules Compendium (p. 112)",
