    id: "rc-senses",
    name: "Senses",
    section: "S",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "Most creatures can see and hear using the Listen and Spot skills, focus attention using Search, and have other senses that come into play from time to time. If it becomes important whether a creature actually senses something specific when smelling, tasting, or touching, a Wisdom check suffices, with the DC set reasonably based on the sensation's strength; Knowledge checks or Appraise checks might be appropriate supplements, depending on what the creature is trying to find out. LISTEN (Wis): every time a creature has a chance to hear something, it can make a Listen check without using an action; trying to hear something it failed to hear previously is a move action. A Listen check is made against a DC reflecting how quiet the noise is, or opposed by another creature's Move Silently check. A successful check lets you tell the general location of or direction to the sound; beating the DC by 10 lets you understand the actual content of the sound, assuming you can otherwise understand it; beating the DC by 20 lets you pinpoint where the sound is coming from. Listen DCs: a battle -10; people talking 0; people whispering 15. DC modifiers (pick the one distance modifier that most applies): ambient noise +5; loud ambient noise +10; extremely loud ambient noise +15; through a door +5; through a stone wall +15; per 10 feet of distance +1; per 20 feet of distance over water +1; per 30 feet of distance underwater +1; listener distracted +5; listener sleeping +10 (a successful check awakens the listener); land creature underwater +10 (for nonaquatic creatures without a natural swim speed). SPOT (Wis): every time a creature has a chance to see something, it can make a Spot check without using an action; trying to spot something it failed to see previously is a move action. A Spot check is made against a DC reflecting how hard the thing is to see, or opposed by another creature's Hide check, and a creature must have line of sight to make a Spot check. Spot penalties: -1 per 10 feet of distance; -5 if the spotter is distracted. Other uses: Spot detects a disguise (opposed by a Disguise check) and reads lips — to read lips, the lip reader must be within 30 feet of the speaker, have line of sight to that creature, do nothing other than move at half speed, and understand the speaker's language; the base DC is 15, increased for complex speech or an inarticulate speaker; the DM makes the check in secret; on a success the lip reader understands the general content of a minute's worth of speaking but might miss certain details; on a failure by 4 or less the lip reader cannot make out what is said, and on a failure by 5 or more the lip reader draws an incorrect conclusion. SEARCH (Int): the searcher must be within 10 feet of the object or surface to be searched, and searching a 5-foot-by-5-foot area or a volume of goods 5 feet on a side takes a full-round action that provokes attacks of opportunity. Search DCs: search a full chest to find an item 10; notice a secret door or a simple trap 20; find a difficult nonmagical trap 21 or higher; find a magic trap 25 + the level of the spell; notice a well-hidden secret door 30; find a footprint varies (a successful check can find a sign of a creature's passage but does not let you find or follow a trail — see Track in this file); only a creature with trapfinding can perform a trap-finding Search check and succeed. Extraordinary senses: Blindsight — even if the creature is blind, it maneuvers and fights as well as a sighted creature, out to the range specified in its description; invisibility, darkness, and most kinds of concealment are irrelevant to blindsight, though the creature must have line of effect to a creature or object to discern it; a creature with blindsight usually does not need Spot or Listen checks to notice and locate creatures within range; blindsight does not subject a creature to gaze attacks, does not distinguish color or visual contrast, and cannot be used to read; blinding attacks do not work on creatures using blindsight, while deafening attacks thwart blindsight that relies on hearing; blindsight works underwater but not in a vacuum; unless otherwise noted it is continuous and requires no action, though some forms must be triggered as a free action, in which case the creature gains its benefit only during its turn. Blindsense — the creature notices creatures and objects it cannot see, but without the precision of blindsight, out to the range in its description; it usually does not need Spot or Listen checks to notice and locate creatures within range, provided it has line of effect to them; any opponent the creature cannot see still has total concealment against it, and it still has the normal miss chance when attacking foes that have concealment; conditions that affect visibility still affect the movement of a creature with blindsense, and it is still denied its Dexterity bonus to AC against attacks from creatures it cannot see. Darkvision — the ability to see with no light source at all, out to a range specified for the creature; a creature using darkvision cannot discern colors; the presence of light does not spoil darkvision; darkvision does not let creatures see anything they could not see otherwise, and it subjects a creature to gaze attacks normally. Low-light vision — the creature can see twice as far as normal in dim light, can read with even the tiniest source of light, and can see outdoors on a moonlit night as well as a human can during the day; superior low-light vision allows a creature to see even farther in shadowy illumination, usually four times as far as normal. Scent — the creature detects creatures and tracks by smell, and can identify familiar odors just as humans do familiar sights. Detecting creatures: scent usually detects other creatures within 30 feet; upwind the range is 60 feet, and downwind 15 feet; strong scents (smoke, rotting garbage) can be detected at twice these ranges, and overpowering scents (skunk musk, troglodyte stench) at three times these ranges. Scent detects a creature's presence but not its specific location; noting the direction of the scent is a move action; if a creature with scent moves within 5 feet of a scent's source, it can pinpoint that source. Powerful odors can mask other scents, completely spoiling the ability to properly detect or identify creatures. A creature with the Track feat and the scent ability can follow tracks by smell (see Track). Tremorsense — the creature is sensitive to vibrations in the ground and can automatically pinpoint the location of anything in contact with the ground within range that is not totally still; the sensing creature must also be in contact with the ground, and the creatures sensed must be taking physical actions, including casting spells with somatic components — they do not have to move from place to place to be detected. If no direct path exists through the ground from the sensing creature to the creature sensed, the ability's range defines the maximum distance of the shortest indirect path. Aquatic creatures with tremorsense can also sense the location of creatures moving through water, as long as the sensing creature is in contact with the same water.",
