    id: "rc-sleight-of-hand",
    name: "Sleight of Hand",
    section: "S",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "Sleight of Hand (Dex; trained only; armor check penalty): if you are trained, you can cut a purse and hide it on your person, palm an unattended object, hide a light weapon in your clothing, or perform some feat of legerdemain with an object no larger than a loaf of bread. Although the skill is technically trained only, a creature with no ranks in it can make a Dexterity check to hide an object on its person or to perform any Sleight of Hand task with a DC of 10 or lower. A Sleight of Hand check is normally a standard action. Tasks and DCs: palm a coin-sized object or make a coin disappear — DC 10; lift a small object from a creature — DC 20; any task while observed — opposed by the observer's Spot check; hide an object on your person — opposed by the searcher's Search check +4. When you use the skill under close observation, your check is opposed by the observer's Spot check — the observer's success does not prevent you from performing the act, but keeps you from doing it unnoticed. When you hide an object on your person, your check is opposed by the Search check of anyone frisking you, and the searcher gains a +4 bonus (it is easier to find an object than to hide it). To take something from another creature you must succeed on a DC 20 Sleight of Hand check; the opponent makes a Spot check to detect the attempt, opposed by the same Sleight of Hand result — an opponent that succeeds notices the attempt regardless of whether you got the item. The DM can limit what you can take this way: coin purses and tiny weapons are fair game, but securely worn items such as bracers of armor are not; if the creature knows you are trying to take something from it, it can resist, forcing you to use the disarm or grapple rules instead. You can use Sleight of Hand to entertain as though using the Perform skill (legerdemain, juggling, and the like). Retries are allowed, but after an initial failure, a second attempt against the same target (or while watched by the same observer who noticed the previous attempt) increases the DC for the task by 10. Check modifiers: wearing baggy or heavy clothing (such as a cloak) +2; the object is a dagger +2; the object is extraordinarily small (coin, ring, shuriken) +4; making the check as a move action -20. Dagger surprise: if you palm a dagger during a fight, you can surprise your opponent when it suddenly appears in your hand — you must have a dagger sheathed on your person, must have the Quick Draw feat, and must be holding nothing in your off hand; you must fight the same foe for at least 2 consecutive rounds so the opponent grows used to your empty off hand; at the beginning of your turn during the third round, use Quick Draw to draw the sheathed dagger as a free action with your off hand, then make a Sleight of Hand check opposed by your opponent's Spot check — if you win, your foe is considered flat-footed for the next single attack you make with the dagger now in your off hand; regardless of success, the same foe will not fall for the same trick from you twice in the same encounter. Unobtrusive spellcasting: as part of casting a spell, you can make a Sleight of Hand check to make your verbal and somatic components less obtrusive — muttering magic words under your breath and making gestures surreptitiously — opposed by any observer's Spot check; the observer's success does not prevent you from casting the spell, but keeps you from doing it unnoticed.",
