    id: "rc-special-abilities",
    name: "Special Abilities",
    section: "S",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "Many creatures can use special abilities, classified as extraordinary or natural when they are not magical, and as spell-like or supernatural when a creature or a character using a class feature creates magical effects without being a spellcaster. Using a special ability is often a standard action, but whether it is a standard action, a full-round action, or not an action at all is defined by the ability's description. Extraordinary abilities are not magical, though they might break the laws of physics; they cannot be disrupted in combat as spells can, they usually do not provoke attacks of opportunity, effects or areas that negate or disrupt magic have no effect on them, they are not subject to dispelling, and they function normally in an antimagic field; using one is a free action unless otherwise noted. Extraordinary ability saving throw DC = 10 + 1/2 the creature's HD + the modifier for the ability score on which the ability is based + other modifiers (often racial). Natural abilities are those a creature has because of its physical nature (such as a bird's ability to fly) — abilities not otherwise designated as extraordinary, supernatural, or spell-like; they are rarely identified as natural (that is assumed), and rarely take a distinct action to use (a lion uses its claws as an attack; it does not activate its claws and then attack). Spell-like abilities usually work just like the spell of the same name, though a few are unique and are explained where described. No verbal, somatic, or material components are required to use a spell-like ability, nor a focus or an experience point cost; the user activates it mentally, and armor never affects a spell-like ability's use, even if it resembles an arcane spell with a somatic component. A spell-like ability usually has a limit on how often it can be used, and one usable at will has no use limit. Using one usually takes 1 standard action and provokes attacks of opportunity unless otherwise noted; if it duplicates a spell with a casting time of less than 1 standard action, the spell-like ability has that casting time. A spell-like ability can be disrupted just as a spell can, and can be used defensively with a Concentration check (DC 15 + spell level) — on a failure the ability fails to go off but the attempt counts against its use limit. Spell-like abilities are subject to dispel magic and to spell resistance if the spell they resemble or duplicate is subject to spell resistance; they do not function in areas where magic is suppressed or negated, such as an antimagic field; and they cannot be used to counterspell, nor can they be counterspelled. A designated caster level defines how difficult the creature's spell-like effects are to dispel and any level-dependent variables such as range and duration; the caster level never affects which spell-like abilities the creature has, and is sometimes lower than the level a spellcasting character must have to cast the spell of the same name; if no caster level is specified, it equals the creature's Hit Dice. When a spell-like ability duplicates a spell that works differently by class, a monster's spell-like abilities are presumed to be the sorcerer/wizard versions, then, in order, the cleric, druid, bard, paladin, and ranger versions. Spell-like ability saving throw DC = 10 + the level of the spell the ability resembles or duplicates + the creature's Charisma modifier + other modifiers (often racial). Swift spell-like abilities: using a quickened spell-like ability is a swift action instead of a free action. Supernatural abilities are magical: they do not function in areas where magic is suppressed or negated, such as an antimagic field, but they cannot be disrupted in combat as spells can, are not subject to spell resistance, counterspells, or dispelling by dispel magic, might have a use limit or be usable at will, take a standard action to use unless otherwise noted, do not usually provoke attacks of opportunity, and never require Concentration checks. Unless otherwise noted, a supernatural ability has an effective caster level equal to the creature's Hit Dice. Supernatural ability saving throw DC = 10 + 1/2 the creature's HD + the modifier for the ability score on which the ability is based + other modifiers (often racial). Comparison: extraordinary abilities — not dispelled, no spell resistance applies, function in an antimagic field, no attack of opportunity for use; spell-like abilities — dispelled, spell resistance applies, suppressed in an antimagic field, use provokes attacks of opportunity; supernatural abilities — not dispelled, no spell resistance applies, suppressed in an antimagic field, no attack of opportunity for use. Targeting special abilities: an ability's descriptive text describes how it functions, and targeting a special ability that is an attack works like targeting for the attack it most closely resembles — a cone-shaped burst works just like a spell's cone-shaped burst, while a hurled object works like a ranged attack; many special abilities augment another sort of action as well.",
    source: "Rules Compendium (p. 118)",
