    summary:
      "Spell resistance is the extraordinary ability to avoid being affected by some spells and spell-like abilities; a few spells also grant spell resistance; extraordinary and supernatural abilities, including enhancement bonuses on magic weapons, are not subject to spell resistance; spell resistance does not stack — it overlaps, so the strongest spell resistance applies while it lasts; a spellcaster might have to make a special check to have a spell effect successfully overcome a creature's spell resistance. Creatures gain spell resistance in a variety of ways, and the ability is important enough that monsters usually have their spell resistances noted in their descriptions. Checking spell resistance: to afflict a creature with spell resistance, a spellcaster must make a caster level check (1d20 + caster level) at least equal to the creature's spell resistance; although spell resistance functions much like AC, a roll of a natural 20 on the caster level check does not automatically succeed, and a natural 1 does not automatically fail. Spell resistance never applies to a spell unless the creature with spell resistance is within the spell's range and line of effect. It might not protect against every spell: some spells bypass spell resistance (those whose descriptions say Spell Resistance: no), and other spell effects can sometimes take effect partially or entirely in a different way that is not subject to spell resistance. What if you have SR?: a creature can voluntarily lower its spell resistance at any time as a standard action, and it remains lowered until the beginning of the creature's next turn; spell resistance does not apply if the creature is willing to accept a spell's result — even a creature with a special resistance to magic can suppress this quality; spell resistance never interferes with one's own spells, items, or abilities, so a creature with spell resistance does not need to make a caster level check against its own spell resistance to affect itself; a creature with spell resistance cannot impart this power to others by touching them or by standing in their midst; spell resistance does not apply to any spells the creature already cast. Spells overcome SR only once: a spell or ability that offers spell resistance succeeds or fails against a creature only once for a particular casting — the same spell cast again later must be checked again; similarly, a spellcaster needs to check spell resistance only once per creature with spell resistance, per spell casting; if a spell or effect has multiple effects, the caster checks SR once for all of them. Overlapping spells: if a creature is within the area of several spells that have failed to penetrate its spell resistance (or even if the creature is under the effect of one spell and another spell tries to affect it), the creature gets a separate check against each spell; a creature must make a saving throw against a spell unaffected by its spell resistance as normal.",
