      "Spellcraft (Int; trained only): use this skill to identify spells as they are cast or as they already exist, to learn spells, to prepare wizard spells, and to identify magical properties. Tasks and DCs: identify a spell being cast — DC 15 + spell level; identify a spell already in place (must be able to see the spell's effects) — DC 20 + spell level; learn a spell from a spellbook or scroll — DC 15 + spell level; prepare a spell from a borrowed spellbook — DC 15 + spell level; decipher a written spell (arcane magical writing) — DC 20 + spell level; identify the material components or focus of a spell being cast (no action required) — DC 20 + spell level; determine the properties of a magic item — DC 30 (or as described in item identification); after identifying a spell, recognize its material components and focus — DC 20 + spell level; identify a glyph of warding spell (the extraction garbles this row's DC pairing — it shows figures of 10 + spell level, 20, and 20 + spell level around identifying glyphs of warding, their basic version, and a more powerful glyph, so verify against print before relying on the exact pairing). Retries: in general, you cannot try again on a check to identify or learn a spell; if you fail to identify a spell being cast, you cannot try again until the spell is cast again; if you fail to learn a spell from a spellbook or scroll, you cannot try again until you gain another rank in Spellcraft; you can make a Spellcraft check to identify a glyph of warding only once — if you fail, you cannot try to identify it again. Special: if you are a specialist wizard, you get a +2 bonus on Spellcraft checks to learn or identify spells from your specialty school, and a -5 penalty on checks involving spells from a prohibited school; you cannot learn spells from a prohibited school with Spellcraft. Spellcraft is also the skill for counterspelling identification (see Spellcasting in this file: Spellcraft DC 15 + spell level to identify a spell being cast).",
