    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "Sorcerers and bards cast arcane spells spontaneously — they do not prepare spells in advance; they choose them when they cast them from their spells known. Spell slots: like other spellcasters, sorcerers and bards have a daily limit on spells cast, tracked by spell slots; a sorcerer or bard can cast a spell using any slot of that spell's level or higher that is available; spell slots for spontaneous casters are described in their class descriptions. Spells known: a sorcerer or bard knows a limited number of spells, chosen from the class spell list; a sorcerer or bard can cast any spell he knows, provided he has a spell slot of the appropriate level or higher and the spell is not prohibited to him (such as a spell from a prohibited school); at each level, a sorcerer or bard can also choose to learn a new spell in place of one he already knows — in effect, the sorcerer 'loses' the old spell in exchange for the new one; the new spell must be the same level as the spell being exchanged, and it must be at least two levels lower than the highest-level sorcerer spell the sorcerer can cast; a sorcerer can swap only a single spell at any given level, and must choose whether to swap the spell at the same time as he gains new spells known for the level. Metamagic and spontaneous casting: sorcerers and bards, like other spellcasters, can use metamagic feats to enhance their spells; because spontaneous casters do not prepare spells in advance, applying a metamagic feat to a spontaneously cast spell increases its casting time; if a metamagic feat increases a spell's casting time to longer than 1 full-round action, it takes an extra full-round action to cast the spell; the increased casting time does not apply to spells with a casting time of 1 swift action, or to spells cast using the Quicken Spell feat. Higher-level spell slots: a sorcerer or bard can fill a higher-level spell slot with a lower-level spell, just as prepared casters can; the spell's effective level (for save DCs, spell resistance checks, and so on) remains what it would be if cast with a slot of its own level. Spontaneous cure/inflict spells: clerics spontaneously convert prepared spells into cure or inflict spells of equal or lower level (see Spell Preparation in this file for the preparation framework this builds on).",
