      "In normal climates, Medium creatures need at least a gallon of fluids and about a pound of decent food per day to avoid starvation; in very hot climates, creatures need two or three times as much water to avoid dehydration. A Small creature needs half as much as a Medium creature (or one-quarter gallon of water and one-quarter pound of food per day, if Small creatures are held to the same measures); a Large creature needs twice as much as a Medium creature. Going without water: a creature can go without water for 1 day plus a number of hours equal to its Constitution score; after this time, the creature must make a Constitution check each hour (DC 10, +1 for each previous check) or take 1d6 points of nonlethal damage; a creature that takes nonlethal damage from lack of water is fatigued; a creature with nonlethal damage from dehydration cannot recover that damage until it drinks water; a creature reduced to unconsciousness by nonlethal damage from dehydration is dying (see Injury, Healing, and Death in this file). Going without food: a creature can go without food for 3 days, in growing discomfort; after this time, the creature must make a Constitution check each day (DC 10, +1 for each previous check) or take 1d6 points of nonlethal damage; creatures that take nonlethal damage from lack of food are fatigued; nonlethal damage from starvation cannot be recovered until the creature gets food — not even magic that restores hit points heals this damage; a creature reduced to unconsciousness by nonlethal damage from starvation becomes dying (see Injury, Healing, and Death in this file).",
