      "You can use a melee attack with a slashing or bludgeoning weapon to strike a weapon or shield that your opponent is holding, or another object your opponent is carrying or wearing. Initiating: if you attempt to sunder a weapon or shield, you provoke an attack of opportunity from the target of your maneuver (a defender can choose whether or not to take this attack of opportunity). Resolving: you and the defender make opposed attack rolls with your respective weapons; the wielder of a two-handed weapon on a sunder attempt gets a +4 bonus on the attack roll, and the wielder of a light weapon takes a -4 penalty; if the combatants are of different sizes, the larger one gets a bonus on the attack roll of +4 per difference in size category. If you succeed, you deal damage to the weapon or shield normally. If you attempt to sunder something the defender is carrying (but not a weapon or shield), you don't provoke an attack of opportunity unless the attack misses; you use the rules for attacking an object that is carried or worn instead of an opposed attack roll (see Objects in this file). If you fail, you deal no damage to the item and you provoke no attack of opportunity from the target.",
