    id: "rc-turning",
    name: "Turning",
    section: "T",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "Good clerics and paladins and some neutral clerics can channel positive energy to turn or destroy undead; evil clerics and some neutral clerics can channel negative energy to rebuke or command undead; neutral clerics of certain deities derive their power from other sources (such as nature or a divine portfolio) and rebuke, command, or turn creatures other than undead. Turning is a standard action that does not provoke attacks of opportunity and can be attempted a number of times per day equal to 3 + the character's Charisma modifier; influence reaches out to a range of 60 feet, and the turning check is made against the undead closest to the turner first. Line of effect: you must have line of effect to the undead to be turned, and the undead nearest you are affected first. Turning check: the first thing you do is roll a turning check to determine the most powerful undead you can affect; this is a Charisma check (1d20 + your Charisma modifier); a cleric or paladin's turning check result determines the maximum Hit Dice of the undead affected: result 0 or lower — cleric's level - 4; 1-3 — level - 3; 4-6 — level - 2; 7-9 — level - 1; 10-12 — cleric's level; 13-15 — level + 1; 16-18 — level + 2; 19-21 — level + 3; 22 or higher — level + 4. Turning damage: after the turning check, roll 2d6 + your cleric level (or paladin level, if turning as a paladin) + your Charisma modifier; this is the total number of Hit Dice of undead you can turn; if your Charisma score is average or low, it is possible to roll fewer Hit Dice of undead turned than indicated by the turning check result — you may skip over already-affected undead that are not powerful enough to count; you may also turn undead of a lower total Hit Dice value than indicated if you wish. Affected undead: those within 60 feet are affected, starting with the closest; each potential target is affected if its Hit Dice are less than or equal to the turning damage remaining and its Hit Dice are less than or equal to the maximum from the turning check; undead with turn resistance subtract that amount from the Hit Dice used to check if they are affected. Turning effects: turned undead flee from you by the best and fastest means available to them for 10 rounds (1 minute); if they cannot flee, they cower (giving any attack rolls against them a +2 bonus); if you approach within 10 feet of them, however, they overcome being turned and act normally; you can attack turned undead from a distance of more than 10 feet; turned undead cannot attack you or move toward you. Destroying undead: if you have twice as many levels as the undead have Hit Dice, you destroy them instead of turning them. Rebuking: rebuked undead cower in awe (attack rolls against them get a +2 bonus) for 10 rounds (1 minute); if you approach within 10 feet, they can act normally. Commanding: if you have twice as many levels as the undead have Hit Dice, you can command any undead that you would normally rebuke; a commanded undead is under your mental control; you must take a standard action to give mental orders to a commanded undead; at any one time, the total number of undead you can command is limited to your level (cleric level or paladin level if commanding as a paladin); commanded undead do not count against the limit of undead you can animate. Dispelling turning: an evil cleric can dispel the turning effect of a good cleric by making a turning check of his own, as if rebuking undead; the evil cleric makes a turning check with a result that determines the highest Hit Dice undead he can dispel; the dispelling turning check uses the same table as a normal turning check; each undead within 60 feet that has an effective Hit Dice (including turn resistance) less than or equal to the result of the dispelling turning check is freed from the turning effect; the evil cleric rolls turning damage dice to determine the total Hit Dice of undead he can dispel. Bolstering undead: an evil cleric can bolster undead with a turning check, making them harder to turn; the bolstered undead's effective Hit Dice against turning checks becomes the turning check result, rather than the undead's Hit Dice + turn resistance; see the bolstering rules in the turning table — bolstering affects undead with fewer Hit Dice than the turning check result; the effect lasts 10 rounds. Paladins and turning: a paladin turns undead as a cleric of three levels lower (paladin level - 3). How turn resistance works: turn resistance is added to the undead's Hit Dice for the purpose of resisting turning effects; it also applies to checks to bolster them. Evil clerics and undead: undead under the control of an evil cleric gain turn resistance equal to the cleric's Charisma bonus (if any) while they remain under his control; an evil cleric makes a turning check in the same way as a good cleric to rebuke or command undead. Turning other creatures: some clerics can turn or rebuke creatures other than undead (such as elementals or creatures with a particular subtype); such turning works identically to turning undead, substituting the other creature type.",
