      "If you wield a second weapon in your off hand, you can get one extra attack per round with that weapon; you suffer a -6 penalty with your regular attack or attacks with your primary hand and a -10 penalty to the attack with your off hand when you fight this way; you can reduce these penalties in two ways: if your off-hand weapon is light, the penalties are reduced by 2 each (to -4 and -8); the Two-Weapon Fighting feat lessens the primary hand penalty by 2 and the off-hand penalty by 6 (to -4 and -4 normally, or -2 and -2 with a light off-hand weapon). Double weapons: you can use a double weapon to make an extra attack with its off end, as if fighting with two weapons; the penalties apply as if the off-hand weapon were light. Throwing weapons: the same rules apply when you throw a weapon from each hand; treat a dart or shuriken as a light weapon for this purpose, but treat a bolas, javelin, net, or sling as a one-handed weapon. Two-Weapon Fighting feat penalties table (primary hand / off hand): normal penalties -6 / -10; off-hand weapon is light -4 / -8; Two-Weapon Fighting feat -4 / -6; feat and off-hand weapon light -2 / -2. Improved Two-Weapon Fighting: in addition to the standard single extra attack you get with an off-hand weapon, you get a second attack with it, albeit at a -5 penalty. Greater Two-Weapon Fighting: you get a third attack with your off-hand weapon, at a -10 penalty. Two-Weapon Defense: when wielding a double weapon or two weapons (including paired magically enhanced weapons), you gain a +1 shield bonus to your AC; when you are fighting defensively or using the total defense action, this shield bonus increases to +2. Two-Weapon Rend: if you hit an opponent with both your primary hand and off-hand weapons, you deal extra damage equal to 1-1/2 times your Strength bonus plus the base damage of your off-hand weapon; you can rend only once per round per opponent. Multiweapon fighting: a creature with three or more hands can fight with a weapon in each hand; the creature can make one extra attack for each extra hand it has; multiweapon fighting otherwise follows the two-weapon fighting rules, with the Multiweapon Fighting feat reducing the penalties as Two-Weapon Fighting does for two-handed creatures.",
